steps.body:
A binary tree is built from nodes. Each node holds a value and links to at most two children, `left` and `right`. A missing child is `null`.

```javascript
class TreeNode {
  constructor(value, left = null, right = null) {
    this.value = value;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
  }
}
console.log(new TreeNode(7).left); // null, a missing child
```

Trees in this lesson arrive as level-order values on one line, top to bottom and left to right, with `-` for a missing node. The children of index `i` sit at `2 * i + 1` and `2 * i + 2`, so every gap keeps its place: the children of a `-` are written as `-` too, unless they fall past the end of the line. Compare the raw token with `"-"` before calling `Number`, so a negative value such as `-3` still becomes a node.

A depth-first traversal visits every node once. The three orders differ only in when they record the node:

- **preorder**: node, left, right
- **inorder**: left, node, right
- **postorder**: left, right, node

```javascript harness
class TreeNode {
  constructor(value, left = null, right = null) {
    this.value = value;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
  }
}

{{code}}
```

```javascript
function inorder(node, out) {
  if (node === null) {
    return;
  }
  inorder(node.left, out);
  out.push(node.value);
  inorder(node.right, out);
}
const out = [];
inorder(new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(3)), out);
console.log(out.join(" ")); // 1 2 3
```

Pass one array down, push into it, and `join` it when the walk ends.

