steps.body:
Most tree questions have one shape: handle `null`, solve the left subtree, solve the right subtree, then combine the two answers with the current node.

```javascript harness
class TreeNode {
  constructor(value, left = null, right = null) {
    this.value = value;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
  }
}

{{code}}
```

```javascript
function sum(node) {
  if (node === null) {
    return 0;
  }
  return node.value + sum(node.left) + sum(node.right);
}
console.log(sum(new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(3)))); // 6
```

The `null` case returns a neutral value, one that does not change the combined answer. It is `0` for sums and counts, and `-Infinity` for a maximum, because any real value beats it. Returning `0` for a maximum is a classic bug: a tree of negative numbers would report `0`.

- **height**: `1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right))`, where an empty tree has height 0
- **count**: `1 + count(node.left) + count(node.right)`
- **maximum**: `Math.max(node.value, maxValue(node.left), maxValue(node.right))`
- **leaves**: a node with no children counts as 1, otherwise add up the two subtrees

Each function visits every node once, so it runs in O(n). The recursion goes only as deep as the tree is tall. This lesson measures height in nodes, so a single node has height 1. Some books count edges instead and get 0.

