steps.body:
A binary search tree (BST) keeps one rule at every node: every value in its left subtree is smaller and every value in its right subtree is larger. To insert, walk down that rule until you reach a `null` spot and put the new node there. Returning the node lets each call reattach its child, and the outer call gives back the root:

```javascript harness
class TreeNode {
  constructor(value, left = null, right = null) {
    this.value = value;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
  }
}

function insert(node, value) {
  if (node === null) {
    return new TreeNode(value);
  }
  if (value < node.value) {
    node.left = insert(node.left, value);
  } else if (value > node.value) {
    node.right = insert(node.right, value);
  }
  return node;
}

function inorder(node, out) {
  if (node === null) {
    return;
  }
  inorder(node.left, out);
  out.push(node.value);
  inorder(node.right, out);
}

const values = [50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80];
{{code}}
const sorted = [];
inorder(root, sorted);
console.log(`root: ${root.value}`);
console.log(`inorder: ${sorted.join(" ")}`);
```

```javascript
let root = null;
for (const v of values) {
  root = insert(root, v);
}
```

Searching follows the same rule, one level per step, so it costs O(height) instead of checking every node:

```javascript harness
class TreeNode {
  constructor(value, left = null, right = null) {
    this.value = value;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
  }
}

{{code}}
```

```javascript
function contains(node, value) {
  while (node !== null) {
    if (value === node.value) {
      return true;
    }
    node = value < node.value ? node.left : node.right;
  }
  return false;
}
const root = new TreeNode(50, new TreeNode(30), new TreeNode(70));
console.log(contains(root, 70), contains(root, 65)); // true false
```

Two free bonuses: an inorder traversal of a BST visits values in sorted order, and the minimum and maximum are the leftmost and rightmost nodes. The catch is shape. Inserting already sorted values puts every node on one side, so the tree becomes a list with height n and search slows to O(n). Duplicates are simply ignored here.

