steps.body:
Level-order traversal visits the tree top to bottom, one level at a time. It is breadth-first search with nodes instead of grid cells: start the queue with the root, read with a head index, and push each node's children. You do not need a visited set, because a tree has no cycles.

To group nodes by level, record how many nodes are waiting at the start of each round. Exactly that many belong to the current level, and everything they push belongs to the next one:

```javascript harness
class TreeNode {
  constructor(value, left = null, right = null) {
    this.value = value;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
  }
}

const root = new TreeNode(
  50,
  new TreeNode(30, new TreeNode(20), new TreeNode(40)),
  new TreeNode(70, new TreeNode(60), new TreeNode(80)),
);
{{code}}
console.log(`visit order: ${queue.map((node) => node.value).join(" ")}`);
```

```javascript
const queue = [root];
let head = 0;
while (head < queue.length) {
  const size = queue.length - head;
  const level = [];
  for (let k = 0; k < size; k++) {
    const node = queue[head++];
    level.push(node.value);
    if (node.left !== null) {
      queue.push(node.left);
    }
    if (node.right !== null) {
      queue.push(node.right);
    }
  }
  // level holds one level, left to right
}
```

With each level in an array, per-level answers are short: its sum with `reduce`, its leftmost value with `level[0]`, and its rightmost value with `level.at(-1)`, which is what you would see looking at the tree from the right side. Guard against an empty tree before you start the queue.

